    historyContent: {
      origins: "First recorded in 1522 in a poem by John Skelton ('Why not to Court'). It is mentioned by Shakespeare and described in detail by Francis Willughby in his 17th-century 'Book of Games' (c. 1665), which is the only known historical description of the rules.",
      originCountries: [
        'England',
      ],
      timeline: [
        "1522: Earliest reference in Skelton's poem.",
        "1591: Mentioned by Florio in 'Second Fruites'.",
        "1594: Referenced by John Lyly in 'Mother Bombie'.",
        "c. 1665: Rules codified by Francis Willughby in his 'Book of Games'.",
        '18th Century: Popular social entertainment for the English gentry.',
        '19th Century: Largely replaced by Casino and other fishing games.',
        'Present: Studied by card historians as the grandfather of modern fishing games.',
      ],
      evolution: "The game's distinctive 'Lying Down' rule was a precursor to the 'Go Fish' mechanic, but much harsher as it removed the player from the round entirely. The fishing mechanic (capturing cards from a pool) influenced later games like Casino and Scopa.",
      cultural: "The name was used as a double entendre in Elizabethan England for amorous encounters. The Oxford English Dictionary notes it was 'frequently used punningly with reference to sexual intercourse.' The game represents the bawdy humor of Renaissance England.",
    },
