        setup: {
          players: "5 players is the standard historical format (Willughby's rules). Easily adapted for 3-6 players.",
          deck: 'Standard 52-card deck. Ace is low for determining the dealer (lowest card deals).',
          equipment: 'Standard deck and counters for stakes.',
          dealing: 'Deal 8 cards to each player (one at a time, face down). Place the remaining 12 cards face-up on the table as the pool. The pool cards should be clearly visible but not arranged in any particular order.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Capture at least 4 pairs (8 cards) and avoid being the first to 'Lie Down.' Be the last player with cards in hand to win the largest share of the pot.",
          gameplay: "1. **Pre-Play Claims**: Before play begins, check your hand for prials (three of a kind) or mournivals (four of a kind). If you have a prial, you may immediately set down two of those cards as a captured pair, keeping the third in hand. If you hold a mournival, you win all four cards immediately. 2. **Play**: Dealer's left-hand neighbor starts. On your turn: Play ONE card from your hand. Use it to capture either ONE or THREE cards of the same rank from the pool. Alternatively, play a PAIR from your hand to capture a PAIR on the table. 3. **Immediate Pair Claim**: If you hold a matching pair in hand, and another player captures a table card of that rank, you may immediately (even out of turn) add your pair to your won cards. 4. **Lying Down**: When you can no longer captureâ€”whether because you have no cards matching any on the table, or because no table cards remainâ€”you must 'Lie Down': place your entire remaining hand face-up on the table as new pool cards, and exit the round.",
          keyRules: [
            'Pair = Two cards of the same rank (A-A, K-K, etc.)',
            'Prial = Three cards of the same rank (pair royal)',
            'Mournival = Four cards of the same rank',
            'If initial pool contains a mournival, dealer wins all four cards immediately.',
            'Each player puts 2 stakes into the pot; dealer puts 3 stakes.',
            'Last player with cards wins 5p from the pot.',
            'Everyone counts won cards. +/- 1p per 2 cards above or below 8 cards.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Prioritize the central pool. If there is only one card of a rank in the pool, and you have that card, take it immediately! This ensures you don't 'Lie Down' for at least one more turn. Don't play pairs from your hand unless forcedâ€”save them as insurance.",
          intermediate: "Pair Hoarding: If you have a pair in your hand, don't play it immediately. Save it as a 'buffer.' If your turn comes and no table capture is possible, you can play one of your pair cards to take the other from the table in the next turn (assuming it survives). Watch for opponents' mistakesâ€”be ready to claim uncollected pairs or mournivals.",
          advanced: "Table Clearing: If you are the leader, try to leave the pool as 'rank-depleted' as possible. If you take the only 7 on the table, the next player who holds a 7 is much more likely to be forced to 'Lie Down.' Force opponents to use their pair cards early by threatening to capture the table cards they need.",
          tips: [
            'Wait for the dealer to take any mournivals from the pool first. Every card taken by the dealer is one less you can use to stay in the game.',
            "Pay attention to the ranks already captured. If all four Kings are captured, a King in your hand is a 'dead card' that will eventually force you to Lie Down.",
            'The player to your left is your biggest threatâ€”watch what they capture to predict what ranks they no longer hold.',
            "If you have a prial (three of a kind), claim two immediately but keep the third as a 'save' card.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
